<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dezful</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dezful is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khuzestan">Khuzestan</a> province, south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, on the Dez river at the foot of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagros-mountains">Zagros</a> mountains. It grew around an ancient bridge across the river, built in the Sasanian period, from which it takes its name. Nearby lie the ruins of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chogha-zanbil">Chogha Zanbil</a>, and the surrounding region was part of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dezful.jpg" alt="Dezful" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mansor110 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D8%A2%D8%B1%D8%A7%D9%85%DA%AF%D8%A7%D9%87_%DB%8C%D8%B9%D9%82%D9%88%D8%A8_%D9%84%DB%8C%D8%AB_%D8%B5%D9%81%D8%A7%D8%B1%DB%8C%D8%8C%D8%A7%D9%88%D9%84%DB%8C%D9%86_%D8%B4%D9%87%D8%B1%DB%8C%D8%A7%D8%B1_%D8%A7%DB%8C%D8%B1%D8%A7%D9%86%DB%8C_%D8%A8%D8%B9%D8%AF_%D8%A7%D8%B2_%D8%A7%D8%B3%D9%84%D8%A7%D9%857.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Dezful lies at the foot of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagros-mountains">Zagros Mountains</a>, about 155 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahvaz">Ahvaz</a>, the provincial capital, and 650 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a>, on the main north-south highway to Ahvaz. The area around it has been home to civilisations for about 5,000 years. Its people speak Dezfuli, a dialect distinct to the city, and Shushtari, sometimes considered one of the most archaic dialects of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iranian-plateau">Iranian plateau</a>. The name comes from Persian words for fortress and bridge; the original form was Dezhpul, changed after the Muslim conquest because Arabic lacks the sounds p and zh.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>According to excavations by Walther Hinz, Awan, capital of the first dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a>, lay at Dezful. The name is believed to come from a Sasanian bridge over the Ab-e Dez built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapur-ii">Shapur II</a>, beside which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a> built a fortress. The area grew into the city, though the name was not commonly used until the 12th century. The stone foundation of the bridge is still visible, while its upper part was rebuilt in brick during the early Islamic, Saljuq and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> periods; in the 14th century Hamd-Allah Mostawfi described it as 520 paces long, with forty-two arches.</p><p>The Ranashi Dynasty was centred at Dezful until its fall in 1542/3 and was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a> from 1508 to 1514. A Mandaean community lived there for many centuries, and the Shia jurist Morteza Ansari was born there in 1781. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-curzon">George Curzon</a> visited in the late 19th century the principal industry was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigo">indigo</a>, with 120 factories, and the city was known for fine reed pens.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Dezful is the capital of Dezful County in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khuzestan">Khuzestan</a> province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, with a population of 264,709 in the 2016 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugarcane">Sugarcane</a> has been grown in the region for more than a thousand years, and the Dezful reed pen has been made there for about three hundred years.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dezful” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Awan was a highland district of western Iran, probably north of Susa near modern <b>Dezful</b> or in Luristan, whose exact site is unknown.</blockquote><a href="/state/awan-dynasty">Awan dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
